An onion service carries a pancake session over
[Tor](https://www.torproject.org/). A peer is named by a version 3 service id,
the 56 base32 characters in `ocapn://<serviceid>.onion`. Bytes travel a Tor
circuit: encrypted, with both ends' network location hidden.

This is the only transport the OCapN draft standardizes, and the only netlayer
upstream [Spritely Goblins](https://spritely.institute/goblins/) ships. Every
other live Goblins check (cleartext `tcp`, and `iroh`) runs over an adapter
this repository wrote for the vat. Over Tor neither side wrote the other's
transport; both follow the draft. `zig build goblins-onion` is that meeting.

## pancake starts no Tor

A deployment runs a Tor daemon; pancake talks to it. You give the netlayer two
endpoints, each an absolute Unix socket path or a `host:port`:

- the **SOCKS endpoint**, which pancake dials through, and
- the **control endpoint**, which pancake asks for a service when it hosts.

Leave the control endpoint out and the netlayer can dial but not host; it says
so rather than reporting an address nothing can reach. The control endpoint may
want a cookie file's bytes or a password; pancake sends either.

Hosting mints a version 3 service and reports the service id partners dial, plus
the key that restores that address after a restart. Store the key and pass it
back to keep one address across restarts.

## First listen

This is the Netlayer surface, not CapTP `Session`. CapTP rides pancake's Host
over the accepted byte channel once you have a connection.

```=html
<!-- site-examples:onion GENERATED by `zig build gen-site-examples`; edits are overwritten -->
```
> []($block.attrs('code-tabs'))
> **ts**
> ```ts
> import { Netlayer } from "@pancake/stack/transport/onion";
>
> // pancake starts no Tor. Name an already-running daemon's SOCKS (and control to host).
> using netlayer = new Netlayer({
>   socks: "/run/tor/socks",
>   control: "/run/tor/control",
> });
> using listener = await netlayer.listen();
> console.log(`ocapn://${listener.serviceId}.onion`); // Goblins-standard locator
> using connection = await listener.accept();
> // Frame I/O here; CapTP rides pancake's Host over this byte channel.
> ```
> **python**
> ```python
> from pancake.onion import OnionNetlayer
>
> # pancake starts no Tor. Name an already-running daemon's SOCKS (and control to host).
> with OnionNetlayer(socks="/run/tor/socks", control="/run/tor/control") as netlayer:
>     listener = netlayer.listen()
>     print(f"ocapn://{listener.service_id}.onion")  # Goblins-standard locator
>     connection = listener.accept()
>     # Frame I/O here; CapTP rides pancake's Host over this byte channel.
> ```
> **rust**
> ```rust
> use pancake::onion::{Auth, Config, Netlayer};
> use pancake::Codec;
>
> // pancake starts no Tor. Name an already-running daemon's SOCKS (and control to host).
> let config = Config::dial_only("/run/tor/socks")
>     .with_control("/run/tor/control", Auth::None);
> let netlayer = Netlayer::new(&config)?;
> let listener = netlayer.listen()?;
> println!("ocapn://{}.onion", listener.service_id()); // Goblins-standard locator
> let _connection = listener.accept(Codec::Syrup, 4096)?;
> // Frame I/O here; CapTP rides pancake's Host over this byte channel.
> ```
> **racket**
> ```scheme
> (require "pancake-onion.rkt")
>
> ;; pancake starts no Tor. Name an already-running daemon's SOCKS (and control to host).
> (define netlayer (make-onion-netlayer #:socks "/run/tor/socks"
>                                       #:control "/run/tor/control"))
> (define listener (onion-netlayer-listen netlayer))
> (printf "ocapn://~a.onion\n" (onion-listener-service-id listener)) ; Goblins-standard locator
> (define connection (onion-listener-accept listener))
> ;; Frame I/O here; CapTP rides pancake's Host over this byte channel.
> ```
