## A service id is an address, not a name for the peer

Tor proves the far end of the circuit holds the key the address encodes. That
is more than a TCP address gives you, and enough to know the bytes reached the
service you meant. It does not say who the CapTP peer is: the peer is whoever
holds the local socket that service forwards to.

The accepting side learns nothing. Tor tells a hidden service nothing about who
dialed it. CapTP does not decide what a caller may do by asking who called; the
caller either holds the reference or it does not. Tor reaches the same place
from the privacy side.

Three-party handoff is the exception: it keys on the location a peer declared,
and an accepted circuit has nothing to check that declaration against. pancake
reports such a location as unproven. Only code that authenticated an end-to-end
channel may report a proved peer. An
[iroh]($link.page('transports/iroh')) session can, because QUIC authenticated
the node id.

## An onion locator carries no hints

Nothing rides beside the designator. The service id already says where to dial,
and the pinned Goblins refuses a hinted onion locator, so pancake emits none.
Hints are also where a peer would state a preferred wire profile, so two
pancake peers over Tor stay on the `1.0` baseline even when both could speak
more.
[ADR-0108](https://github.com/jimisaacs/pancake/blob/main/docs/decisions/adr/ADR-0108-onion-netlayer.md)
records that.

[Choosing a transport]($link.page('transports/choosing')) puts onion beside the
others. [What you can depend on]($link.page('stability')) lists the live Tor
checks and what still sits beside ordinary `zig build check`.
